<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en-GB">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Get your business ready to sell | Arrington Consultancy</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Practical commercial work to make an established business stronger, less dependent on its owner and straightforward for somebody else to take on. Starts with a conversation or a £500 Commercial Review.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.arringtonconsultancy.com/get-your-business-ready-to-sell">
    <meta property="og:type" content="website">
    <meta property="og:title" content="Get your business ready to sell | Arrington Consultancy">
    <meta property="og:description" content="A business that can run without you is easier for somebody else to take on, and better to own while you still have it.">
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://www.arringtonconsultancy.com/get-your-business-ready-to-sell">
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://www.arringtonconsultancy.com/img/logo">
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="Get your business ready to sell | Arrington Consultancy">
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="A business that can run without you is easier for somebody else to take on, and better to own while you still have it.">
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://www.arringtonconsultancy.com/img/logo">
    <meta name="csrf-token" content="<%= csrfToken %>">
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="any">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,700;1,400&family=DM+Serif+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
    <%# The site-wide Ads/GA4 base tag every other page carries. No new
        conversion action and no new label is introduced here: the only
        conversions this page can fire are the two existing site-wide ones in
        site-chrome-script (contact click, contact form submit), exactly as on
        every other page. Tom's build instruction of 25/09/2026 is that Google
        Ads campaigns and settings are not changed by this build, and they are
        not. %>
    <script async src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=AW-18129914078"></script>
    <script nonce="<%= nonce %>">
        window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
        function gtag(){dataLayer.push(arguments);}
        gtag('js', new Date());
        gtag('config', 'AW-18129914078');
        <% if (ga4Id) { %>
        gtag('config', '<%= ga4Id %>');
        <% } %>
    </script>
    <%# Deliberately NO Product/Offer schema partial here, unlike the four
        Where to Start offer pages. Those describe a purchasable offer at a
        published price. This page introduces no new priced offer, so marking
        one up would assert a product that does not exist. The £500 Commercial
        Review keeps its own schema on its own page. %>
    <style nonce="<%= nonce %>">
        :root {
            <% for (const [prop, val] of Object.entries(theme.vars)) { %>
            <%= prop %>: <%= val %>;
            <% } %>
        }
        * { box-sizing: border-box; }
        body { margin: 0; font-family: 'DM Sans', sans-serif; background: var(--black); color: var(--text); line-height: 1.6; }
        <%- include('partials/site-chrome-styles') %>

        h1, h2, h3 { font-family: 'DM Serif Display', serif; font-weight: 400; color: var(--text-bright); line-height: 1.2; margin: 0; }
        p { color: var(--text); margin: 0 0 1.2rem; }

        .btn { display: inline-block; font-family: 'DM Sans', sans-serif; font-size: 0.92rem; font-weight: 600; padding: 0.6rem 1.5rem; border-radius: 3px; text-decoration: none; cursor: pointer; border: 1px solid transparent; }
        .btn-primary { background: var(--accent); color: var(--black); border-color: var(--accent); }
        .btn-primary:hover, .btn-primary:focus-visible { background: var(--accent-hover); border-color: var(--accent-hover); }
        /* Same reasoning as the Commercial Review page: the generic outline
           border is dim enough on the near-black ground to read as a disabled
           box, so the secondary route carries the accent at rest. The fill is
           what separates it from the primary CTA, which is the site's existing
           button language rather than a new one. */
        .btn-outline { background: transparent; color: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); }
        .btn-outline:hover, .btn-outline:focus-visible { background: var(--accent); color: var(--black); }
        /* No min-height or flex centring is declared for the buttons here on
           purpose. site-chrome-styles already sets both site-wide via
           `a.btn, button.btn`, element-qualified so it deliberately outranks
           any page's own bare `.btn`, and measuring the rendered page confirms
           it reaches these buttons (44px, flex-centred). A 48px rule here
           would be dead code: it loses that cascade, so the comment beside it
           would describe a height the page does not have. Both routes are
           therefore the same height already, and the difference in weight is
           the fill rather than the size. */

        main { max-width: 640px; margin: 0 auto; padding: calc(var(--site-chrome) + 2.5rem) 1.5rem 5rem; }

        .sr-label { font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.14em; font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 1rem; display: block; }
        h1 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4.5vw, 2.4rem); margin-bottom: 1.4rem; }
        .sr-lede { font-size: 1.12rem; color: var(--text-bright); }

        /* The same bordered-card device the Where to Start offer pages and
           Owner Check use, so this page reads as part of the site rather than
           a campaign microsite. Related facts sit together in one card with a
           lighter internal rule between items, not a divider after every
           paragraph. */
        .sr-card { border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px; padding: 2rem 2.25rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
        .sr-card-item + .sr-card-item { margin-top: 1.75rem; padding-top: 1.75rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }
        .sr-card h2 { font-size: 1.05rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
        .sr-card h3 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
        .sr-card p { font-size: 1.02rem; }
        .sr-card p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
        .sr-card.surface-paper { background: var(--surface-paper); }

        .sr-list { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.3rem; color: var(--text); }
        .sr-list li { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; font-size: 1.02rem; }
        .sr-list li:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

        .sr-section { margin-top: 3rem; }
        .sr-section > h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }

        .sr-route-actions { margin-top: 1.2rem; }
        .sr-final { border-top: 1px solid var(--border); padding-top: 2.5rem; margin-top: 3.5rem; }
        .sr-final h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
        .sr-final-actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.9rem; margin-top: 1.6rem; }
        .sr-aside { font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 1.6rem; }
        /* An inline link in body copy. Nothing in site-chrome-styles sets a
           colour for one, so without this the Evidence link rendered in
           default browser blue and underlined on the near-black ground, which
           reads as an unstyled page rather than an Arrington one. Found by
           looking at the rendered page on a phone, not in the markup. This is
           the site's existing treatment, copied from .case-more a in
           index.ejs rather than invented here: accent, no underline, and a
           faint accent rule that solidifies on hover. */
        .sr-aside a {
            color: var(--accent);
            text-decoration: none;
            border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 40%, transparent);
            padding-bottom: 2px;
        }
        .sr-aside a:hover, .sr-aside a:focus-visible { border-bottom-color: var(--accent); }

        @media (max-width: 520px) {
            .sr-card { padding: 1.75rem 1.5rem; }
            .sr-final-actions .btn { width: 100%; }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <%# currentPage carries a slug that is deliberately not in navPages: this
        is a campaign landing page and is not in the main navigation (Tom's
        decision of 25/09/2026), so nothing in the nav should highlight as
        active. The header and footer are otherwise the site's own, which is
        what the Build Protocol's assessment/tool rule requires. %>
    <%- include('partials/site-header', { currentPage: { slug: 'get-your-business-ready-to-sell' }, navPages, canEdit: false, content, pageContact }) %>

    <main>
        <span class="sr-label">Sale readiness</span>
        <h1>Get your business ready to sell</h1>
        <p class="sr-lede">A business that can run without you is easier for somebody else to take on, and better to own while you still have it. That is the work: strengthening how the business runs, improving what it earns, and reducing how much of it depends on you being there.</p>

        <div class="sr-section">
            <h2>Where you are now</h2>
            <p>These are two different situations and they need different first steps, so it is worth being honest about which one you are in.</p>

            <div class="sr-card">
                <div class="sr-card-item">
                    <h3>You are preparing ahead</h3>
                    <p>A sale is somewhere in mind, perhaps a few years out, and nothing is under way. This is the better position to be in, because most of what makes a business straightforward to hand over takes time to put right. Start with a Commercial Review.</p>
                    <div class="sr-route-actions">
                        <a href="/where-to-start/commercial-review" class="btn btn-outline" id="srPrepareCta">See the £500 Commercial Review</a>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="sr-card-item">
                    <h3>You are already selling</h3>
                    <p>There is an offer on the table, or conversations are under way. Speak to us before buying anything. Some of what a buyer will ask about cannot be put right inside a live process, and we would rather say so on a call than take a fee for a review that tells you the same thing.</p>
                    <div class="sr-route-actions">
                        <a href="/book-a-30-minute-conversation" class="btn btn-primary" id="srSellingCta">Book a 30 minute conversation</a>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-section">
            <h2>What a buyer will want to understand</h2>
            <p>None of this is exotic. It is the same short list of questions every time, and most owner run businesses have good answers to some of it and no answer at all to the rest.</p>
            <ul class="sr-list">
                <li>How much of the day to day runs through you personally, and what would happen in the fortnight after you stopped.</li>
                <li>Whether the records would hold up when an accountant goes through them properly.</li>
                <li>How much of the revenue sits with one or two customers or a single supplier.</li>
                <li>Which work makes money and which only looks busy.</li>
                <li>What is written down, and what exists only as habit in somebody's head.</li>
                <li>The things you have known needed fixing for a long time and have not got to.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-section">
            <h2>What the Commercial Review establishes</h2>
            <p>We start with the same Commercial Review we run for any owner. What changes here is what we are looking for.</p>
            <div class="sr-card">
                <div class="sr-card-item">
                    <h3>The conversation</h3>
                    <p>We listen for as long as it genuinely takes to understand the business, whether that is thirty minutes or two hours. You know your business better than we will; the value we add is an outside commercial read on it.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="sr-card-item">
                    <h3>The written assessment</h3>
                    <p>Within 4 business days you get a written assessment: where the business depends on you and what that would cost a new owner, which records would not survive scrutiny, where margin is actually made, and what should change first rather than a list of everything that could change.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="sr-card-item">
                    <h3>The price</h3>
                    <p>£500. If you go on to a larger review route afterwards, that £500 is credited in full against it.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-section">
            <h2>What the work can involve afterwards</h2>
            <p>Once the review has established what matters, we can get involved in putting it right rather than handing over a document and leaving. What that means in practice depends on the business, but it is usually some of the following.</p>
            <ul class="sr-list">
                <li>Moving decisions and responsibilities off you, with the people who will actually hold them.</li>
                <li>Reviewing pricing where the margin does not reflect the work.</li>
                <li>Turning the decisions you make by instinct into something written down that survives a handover.</li>
                <li>Management information you do not have to assemble yourself every month.</li>
                <li>Reducing how much rests on one customer, one supplier or one member of staff.</li>
                <li>Systems, a shared workspace or AI, where they carry one of the changes above. The commercial problem comes first and the technology follows from it, never the other way round.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="sr-aside">That work is agreed separately once we both know what it involves. There is no standing package price for it, because the work is different in every business, and it is not a retainer.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-section">
            <h2>What we do not do</h2>
            <div class="sr-card surface-paper">
                <p>We are not a broker, a valuer or an exit planner, and this is not advice on whether you should sell. We do not find buyers, introduce you to them or negotiate on your behalf. We do not carry out formal valuations, legal drafting, tax structuring or specialist accounting work.</p>
                <p>Where any of that is needed we will say so plainly, and work alongside the accountant or solicitor you appoint. We make no claim about what the business would sell for, and no promise that it will sell at all.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-section">
            <h2>How you would know it worked</h2>
            <p>Before the work starts we agree which of these matter to you, so there is something to check against afterwards rather than an impression that things feel better.</p>
            <ul class="sr-list">
                <li>How many decisions still have to come through you.</li>
                <li>The time you spend on work only you can do.</li>
                <li>The share of revenue sitting with your largest customer.</li>
                <li>Margin by type of work, rather than one figure for everything.</li>
                <li>Which records an accountant would now accept without rebuilding them.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-final">
            <h2>Start with a conversation</h2>
            <p>Thirty minutes, no charge. Tell us where the business is and what you are thinking, and we will say plainly whether this is work worth doing yet, and what it would involve if it is.</p>
            <div class="sr-final-actions">
                <a href="/book-a-30-minute-conversation" class="btn btn-primary" id="srPrimaryCta">Book a 30 minute conversation</a>
                <a href="/where-to-start/commercial-review" class="btn btn-outline" id="srSecondaryCta">Or start with the £500 Commercial Review</a>
            </div>
            <%# Evidence is LINKED rather than summarised, and nothing on this
                page describes an existing case as sale-readiness work. Tom's
                instruction of 25/09/2026: do not reframe an existing case as a
                sale-readiness case study unless the controlled evidence
                specifically supports that description. No such controlled
                evidence has been inspected, so the honest move is to point at
                the proof that exists rather than recast it. %>
            <p class="sr-aside">Twenty years of running and fixing businesses across Devon and Cornwall sits behind this. <a href="/evidence">See what we have done</a>.</p>
        </div>
    </main>

    <%- include('partials/site-footer', { canEdit: false, pageContact, content }) %>
    <script nonce="<%= nonce %>">
        <%- include('partials/site-chrome-script') %>
    </script>
</body>
</html>
